\par \b Red/Blue Coincidence Analysis Pipeline\b0\par \par This document explains how to use the Python script for batch analysis of red/blue feature coincidence in paired TIFF images (Opera Phenix data). It assumes filenames like:\par \par

\tt ...-ch1....tif \up0 = red channel\par
\tt ...-ch2....tif \up0 = blue channel\par \par The script:\par \par
Pairs ch1 and ch2 images in each folder\par
Detects spots in red and blue\par
Quantifies coincidence between channels\par
Computes densities (per \u181?m\u178?)\par
Computes per-spot metrics and coincidence flags\par
Saves per-image results and per-folder/global summaries\par \par \par \b 1. Requirements\b0\par \par You need Python 3 and the following packages:\par \par
numpy\par
pandas\par
scikit-image (skimage)\par
matplotlib (not strictly required in current version, but often installed with scikit-image)\par
scipy (not used for plotting now, but may be present from earlier versions)\par \par Install them (if needed) with:\par \par \pard\li720\tt pip install numpy pandas scikit-image\par \pard\li0\par If you use conda:\par \par \pard\li720\tt conda install numpy pandas scikit-image\par \pard\li0\par \par \b 2. File and folder structure\b0\par \par \b 2.1 Input images\b0\par \par The script expects one file per channel:\par \par
Red (ch1): e.g.\par \tt r02c07f01p01-ch1sk1fk1fl1.tiff\par
Blue (ch2): e.g.\par \tt r02c07f01p01-ch2sk1fk1fl1.tiff\par \par For each red file, the script looks for the corresponding blue file by replacing "ch1" with "ch2" in the filename.\par \par Images must:\par \par
Be 2D grayscale TIFFs (no channel dimension inside the file).\par
Have matching shapes for red and blue in each pair.\par \par \b 2.2 Data folders (pathList)\b0\par \par You define a list of folders to analyse, for example:\par \par \pard\li720\tt pathList = [\par r"/Volumes/T7/010526_Sample_Panel_OperaPhenix /RawData_EV/Test/",\par r"/Volumes/T7/010526_Sample_Panel_OperaPhenix /RawData_EV/Test2/",\par ]\par \pard\li0\par Each folder in pathList:\par \par
Contains TIFF files with ch1 and ch2 in their names.\par
Will get:\par
One *_results subfolder per image pair.\par
A per-folder image_summary_metrics.tsv.\par
A per-folder global_mean_density_intensity.tsv.\par \par \b 2.3 Root folder (top_root_directory)\b0\par \par You also set a "root" directory where the script writes global summary files:\par \par \pard\li720\tt top_root_directory = r"/Volumes/T7/010526_Sample_Panel_OperaPhenix /RawData_EV/"\par \pard\li0\par This root will receive:\par \par
image_summary_metrics.tsv (all image pairs from all folders combined)\par
summary_CV_metrics.tsv\par
global_mean_density_intensity.tsv\par
per_folder_count_and_density_stats.tsv\par \par Make sure the path (including spaces) exactly matches the real folder name.\par \par \b 3. What the script does (conceptual overview)\b0\par \par For each folder in pathList:\par \par
Finds all red (ch1) TIFF files.\par
For each red file, finds the matching blue (ch2) file.\par
Loads red and blue images.\par
Optionally crops to a rectangular ROI.\par
Thresholds each channel (fixed value, Otsu, or Yen).\par
Labels spots in each binary mask.\par
Computes:\par
Red spot count\par
Blue spot count\par
Number of red spots overlapping blue (coincident)\par
Fraction of red spots that are coincident\par
Densities (counts per \u181?m\u178?) for red, blue, and coincident spots\par
A rotated / translated blue control and its coincidence with red\par
Computes per-spot properties for each channel:\par
Centroid (x, y)\par
Area (pixels and \u181?m\u178?)\par
Sum / mean / max intensity\par
Whether each spot is coincident or not\par
Saves:\par
Binary images (red_binary.tif, blue_binary.tif)\par
Per-spot CSVs (red_spots.csv, blue_spots.csv)\par
One row of summary metrics per image pair.\par \par After processing all folders and image pairs, it:\par \par
Combines all rows into a global table.\par
Writes global summary files in top_root_directory.\par
Writes per-folder summary files back into each data folder.\par \par \b 4. Key parameters you may want to edit\b0\par \par Near the top of the script you’ll see the user parameters.\par \par \b 4.1 Pixel size\b0\par \par \pard\li720\tt pixel_size = 591 # nm\par \pard\li0\par
Pixel size in nanometres.\par
Used to calculate area in \u181?m\u178? for densities and spot areas.\par
If your imaging settings change, update this value.\par \par \b 4.2 Root directory and data folders\b0\par \par \pard\li720\tt top_root_directory = r".../RawData_EV/"\par \par pathList = [\par r".../RawData_EV/Test/",\par r".../RawData_EV/Test2/",\par ]\par \pard\li0\par
top_root_directory is where global TSVs are written.\par
pathList contains folders to analyse.\par
Paths with spaces must be exact.\par \par \b 4.3 Thresholding mode\b0\par \par \pard\li720\tt THRESHOLD_MODE = "fixed" # or "otsu" or "yen"\par RED_FIXED_THRESHOLD = 200\par BLUE_FIXED_THRESHOLD = 200\par \pard\li0\par
"fixed": use the given numeric thresholds.\par
"otsu": compute threshold per image using Otsu’s method.\par
"yen": compute threshold per image using Yen’s method.\par \par If you use "otsu" or "yen", the RED_FIXED_THRESHOLD and BLUE_FIXED_THRESHOLD values are ignored.\par \par \b 4.4 ROI (Region of Interest)\b0\par \par \pard\li720\tt USE_ROI = False\par ROI_X = (20, 1060) # (xmin, xmax) in pixels\par ROI_Y = (20, 1060) # (ymin, ymax) in pixels\par \pard\li0\par
If USE_ROI = False, the script analyses the full image.\par
If USE_ROI = True, each image is cropped to the given rectangle.\par
ROI is clipped to stay within image bounds.\par \par \b 5. Outputs\b0\par \par \b 5.1 Per-image / per-pair outputs\b0\par \par For each red/blue image pair, a folder like:\par \par <that_folder>/<base_name>_results/\par \par is created, containing:\par \par
red_binary.tif\par
Binary mask (0/255) of red spots after thresholding.\par
blue_binary.tif\par
Binary mask (0/255) of blue spots after thresholding.\par
red_spots.csv\par
One row per red spot with columns:\par
label_id\par
centroid_y, centroid_x\par
area_pixels, area_um2\par
sum_intensity, mean_intensity, max_intensity\par
is_coincident (1 if this red spot overlaps the blue mask)\par
blue_spots.csv\par
Same columns as red_spots.csv, but for blue spots, and is_coincident indicates overlap with the red mask.\par \par \b 5.2 Per-image summary table (global)\b0\par \par In top_root_directory:\par \par
image_summary_metrics.tsv\par \par This file has one row per image pair (per folder), with columns such as:\par \par
Paths and names:\par
red_image_path, blue_image_path, red_image_name, blue_image_name, folder, roi_info\par
Thresholds:\par
red_threshold, blue_threshold, threshold_mode\par
Counts:\par
red_count, blue_count, coincident_count,\par
coincident_count_rot90px1000 (control)\par
fraction_red_coincident\par
Densities (per \u181?m\u178?):\par
red_density_per_um2, blue_density_per_um2\par
coincident_density_per_um2\par
coincident_density_rot90px1000_per_um2\par
Basic intensity metrics:\par
red_spot_sum_mean, red_spot_sum_sd\par
blue_spot_sum_mean, blue_spot_sum_sd\par \par \b 5.3 Global summary tables (root)\b0\par \par In top_root_directory you also get:\par \par
summary_CV_metrics.tsv\par
One row per metric (e.g. red_density_per_um2, blue_spot_sum_mean).\par
Columns:\par
metric\par
mean_across_image_pairs\par
sd_across_image_pairs\par
cv_across_image_pairs\par \par
global_mean_density_intensity.tsv\par
One row per metric (e.g. red_count, red_density_per_um2, red_spot_sum_mean).\par
Columns:\par
metric\par
global_mean\par
global_sd\par \par
per_folder_count_and_density_stats.tsv\par
One row per input folder (per pathList entry).\par
Columns include:\par
folder\par
red_count_mean, red_count_sd, red_count_cv\par
blue_count_mean, blue_count_sd, blue_count_cv\par
coincident_count_mean, coincident_count_sd, coincident_count_cv\par
red_density_per_um2_mean, red_density_per_um2_sd, red_density_per_um2_cv\par
blue_density_per_um2_mean, ...\par
coincident_density_per_um2_mean, ...\par
coincident_density_rot90px1000_per_um2_mean, ...\par \par \b 5.4 Per-folder summary files (inside each data folder)\b0\par \par Inside each folder in pathList, e.g.:\par \par .../RawData_EV/Test/\par .../RawData_EV/Test2/\par \par you will also get:\par \par
image_summary_metrics.tsv\par
Same structure as the global file, but only rows for that specific folder.\par \par
global_mean_density_intensity.tsv\par
Means and SDs computed only over that folder’s images.\par \par \b 6. How to run the script\b0\par \par
Open a terminal.\par
Navigate to the folder containing your script, for example:\par \par \pard\li720\tt cd /path/to/your/script\par \pard\li0\par
Make sure the script is executable (optional):\par \par \pard\li720\tt chmod +x red_blue_coincidence.py\par \pard\li0\par
Run the script with Python:\par \par \pard\li720\tt python red_blue_coincidence.py\par \pard\li0\par or, if executable and with a proper shebang:\par \par \pard\li720\tt ./red_blue_coincidence.py\par \pard\li0\par \par While running, you’ll see messages like:\par \par
Processing pair: red=..., blue=...\par
Analysing region: ...\par
X features were detected in the Red ROI.\par
Updates about summary files being written.\par \par \b 7. Common adjustments\b0\par \par \b 7.1 Changing thresholds\b0\par \par If you find you are over- or under-detecting spots:\par \par
To try Otsu automatically:\par \par \pard\li720\tt THRESHOLD_MODE = "otsu"\par \pard\li0\par
To refine fixed thresholds:\par \par \pard\li720\tt THRESHOLD_MODE = "fixed"\par RED_FIXED_THRESHOLD = 150 # example\par BLUE_FIXED_THRESHOLD = 180\par \pard\li0\par \par \b 7.2 Changing ROI\b0\par \par If you want to exclude borders or artefacts:\par \par \pard\li720\tt USE_ROI = True\par ROI_X = (100, 900)\par ROI_Y = (100, 900)\par \pard\li0\par \par Make sure the ROI is within the image dimensions.\par \par \b 7.3 Changing rotation control\b0\par \par If you want a different control transformation (e.g. 180° rotation, different shift):\par \par \pard\li720\tt blue_rt = rotate_translate_mask_wrap(\par blue_binary,\par angle_deg=180,\par shift_x=500,\par shift_y=0\par )\par \pard\li0\par \par \b 8. Interpreting the key metrics\b0\par \par
red_count, blue_count:\par
Number of detected spots per image in each channel.\par \par
coincident_count:\par
Number of red spots that overlap at least one blue pixel.\par \par
fraction_red_coincident:\par
coincident_count / red_count.\par
Fraction of red spots that are "positive" for blue.\par \par
*_density_per_um2:\par
Counts normalized by ROI area, giving feature density per \u181?m\u178?.\par \par
coincident_density_rot90px1000_per_um2:\par
Coincident density when the blue mask is rotated 90° and shifted by 1000 pixels with wrap-around.\par
Acts as a geometric/random control.\par \par
red_spot_sum_mean, blue_spot_sum_mean:\par
Mean per-spot summed intensity in each channel.\par
Coarse measure of brightness per detected spot.\par \par
red_spots.csv / blue_spots.csv per spot:\par
is_coincident = 1 for spots that overlap the other channel’s binary mask.\par
Useful for exporting to other analysis tools or visualisation.\par \par \b 9. Troubleshooting\b0\par \par
No ch1 files found:\par
Check naming: the script looks for ch1.tif or ch1.tiff.\par
Check that paths in pathList are correct and accessible.\par \par
Shape mismatch warnings:\par
Indicates red and blue images of a pair don’t have exactly the same dimensions.\par
Check the acquisition/export pipeline to ensure both channels are registered and cropped identically.\par \par
"Expected 2D grayscale images" error:\par
Your TIFF might contain multiple channels in one file.\par
This script assumes one channel per file. You’d need to split channels beforehand or modify the script.\par \par
No summary files created:\par
The terminal should print "No image pairs processed; no summary files created."\par
Check that there are matching ch1 and ch2 pairs and that paths are correct.\par \par \b 10. Extending the script\b0\par \par Possible extensions:\par \par
Add more per-spot regionprops (eccentricity, perimeter, major/minor axis).\par
Add QC images that overlay detections on original intensities.\par
Group folders by experimental conditions and compute group-level stats.\par \par If you plan to extend it, the main places to touch are:\par \par
extract_regionprops_table (for more per-spot columns).\par
The lists global_metrics, cv_metrics, and density_metrics (for which summary metrics are computed).\par
The pathList and folder naming conventions (for different experiment layouts).\par \par }
